---
import LearningLayout from '../../../layouts/LearningLayout.astro';
import BulletList from '../../../components/BulletList.astro';
const lb = '{';
const rb = '}';
---

<LearningLayout
  title="Quantum Computing"
  description="From Schrödinger's cat to quantum supremacy — understand the computers that could change everything"
  category="Science"
  categoryHref="/learning/science"
  difficulty="Beginner"
  readTime="28 min read"
>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- PART 1: GENTLE INTRODUCTION                                -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<!-- Section 1: The Hook -->
<section class="mb-12 md:mb-20 fade-in">
  <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border-subtle rounded-xl p-5 md:p-8 text-center">
    <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-text-secondary mb-2">Think of it as a</p>
    <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-primary mb-4">Parallel Universe Computer</p>
    <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm md:text-base max-w-lg mx-auto">
      Instead of checking one possibility at a time, quantum computers exploit the weird rules of physics
      to process information in fundamentally new ways — solving certain problems that would take
      classical computers longer than the age of the universe.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: The Coin Analogy -->
<section class="mb-12 md:mb-20 fade-in">
  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-6 text-white">The Coin Analogy</h2>

  <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border-subtle rounded-xl p-6 md:p-8">
    <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm md:text-base mb-6">
      A <span class="text-primary font-medium">classical bit</span> is like a coin lying on a table — it's either
      heads (1) or tails (0). That's it. Every bit in your phone, your laptop, every server on earth stores
      information this way: definite, certain, one or the other.
    </p>
    <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm md:text-base mb-8">
      A <span class="text-primary font-medium">qubit</span> is like a coin spinning in the air. While it spins,
      it's not heads <em>and</em> tails — it's in a state that has some probability of landing heads and some
      probability of landing tails. Only when it lands (when you <strong class="text-white">measure</strong> it)
      does it become a definite value. This is <span class="text-primary font-medium">superposition</span>.
    </p>

    <!-- SVG: Coin flat vs spinning -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 600 200" class="w-full h-auto min-w-[480px]">
        <!-- Classical Bit -->
        <g>
          <text x="150" y="25" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A3A3A3" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Classical Bit</text>
          <!-- Coin flat - heads -->
          <ellipse cx="100" cy="110" rx="45" ry="12" fill="#1a1a1a" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="2"/>
          <ellipse cx="100" cy="100" rx="45" ry="12" fill="#0a0a0a" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="100" y="105" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fbbf24" font-size="14" font-weight="700">HEADS</text>
          <text x="100" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="11">= 1 (definite)</text>

          <!-- OR -->
          <text x="195" y="105" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="12" font-weight="500">OR</text>

          <!-- Coin flat - tails -->
          <ellipse cx="260" cy="110" rx="45" ry="12" fill="#1a1a1a" stroke="#60a5fa" stroke-width="2"/>
          <ellipse cx="260" cy="100" rx="45" ry="12" fill="#0a0a0a" stroke="#60a5fa" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="260" y="105" text-anchor="middle" fill="#60a5fa" font-size="14" font-weight="700">TAILS</text>
          <text x="260" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="11">= 0 (definite)</text>
        </g>

        <!-- Qubit -->
        <g>
          <text x="470" y="25" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A3A3A3" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Qubit</text>
          <!-- Spinning coin - tilted ellipse with motion lines -->
          <ellipse cx="470" cy="100" rx="20" ry="45" fill="none" stroke="#00FF88" stroke-width="2" transform="rotate(-15 470 100)">
            <animateTransform attributeName="transform" type="rotate" values="-15 470 100;15 470 100;-15 470 100" dur="2s" repeatCount="indefinite"/>
          </ellipse>
          <ellipse cx="470" cy="100" rx="18" ry="42" fill="#0a0a0a" stroke="#00FF88" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.3" transform="rotate(15 470 100)">
            <animateTransform attributeName="transform" type="rotate" values="15 470 100;-15 470 100;15 470 100" dur="2s" repeatCount="indefinite"/>
          </ellipse>
          <!-- Motion arcs -->
          <path d="M 440 60 Q 430 55 435 65" fill="none" stroke="#00FF88" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.4"/>
          <path d="M 500 60 Q 510 55 505 65" fill="none" stroke="#00FF88" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.4"/>
          <path d="M 440 140 Q 430 145 435 135" fill="none" stroke="#00FF88" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.4"/>
          <path d="M 500 140 Q 510 145 505 135" fill="none" stroke="#00FF88" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.4"/>
          <text x="470" y="170" text-anchor="middle" fill="#00FF88" font-size="11">undetermined</text>
          <text x="470" y="185" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="10">until measured</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-4">
      <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm">
        <span class="text-primary font-medium">⚠️ Important:</span> Superposition is <strong class="text-white">not</strong>
        "being in two states at once." It's a mathematical description of a state that has <em>amplitudes</em>
        (complex numbers) associated with each possible outcome. These amplitudes determine the
        <em>probability</em> of each result when measured. The coin analogy is useful but imperfect —
        a spinning coin already "is" one side, we just can't see it. A qubit genuinely has no definite value until observed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Why We Need It -->
<section class="mb-12 md:mb-20 fade-in">
  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-6 text-white">Why Do We Need Quantum Computers?</h2>

  <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm md:text-base mb-6">
    Classical computers are incredible. But some problems grow so explosively that even all the
    computers on Earth working together until the heat death of the universe couldn't solve them.
    These aren't obscure puzzles — they're problems in drug discovery, cryptography, materials science,
    and optimization that affect billions of lives.
  </p>

  <!-- SVG: Exponential scaling -->
  <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 280" class="w-full h-auto min-w-[480px]">
      <text x="300" y="22" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A3A3A3" font-size="13" font-weight="600">The Exponential Wall</text>

      <!-- Axes -->
      <line x1="80" y1="250" x2="560" y2="250" stroke="#404040" stroke-width="1"/>
      <line x1="80" y1="250" x2="80" y2="40" stroke="#404040" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="320" y="275" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="11">Problem Size (n)</text>
      <text x="30" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="11" transform="rotate(-90 30 145)">Time to Solve</text>

      <!-- Classical (exponential) curve -->
      <path d="M 80 248 Q 200 245 300 230 Q 400 180 440 100 Q 460 60 470 42" fill="none" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2.5"/>
      <text x="480" y="45" fill="#ef4444" font-size="11" font-weight="500">Classical: 2ⁿ</text>

      <!-- Quantum (polynomial) curve -->
      <path d="M 80 248 L 200 240 L 300 225 L 400 205 L 500 180 L 560 165" fill="none" stroke="#00FF88" stroke-width="2.5"/>
      <text x="530" y="155" fill="#00FF88" font-size="11" font-weight="500">Quantum</text>

      <!-- Annotation -->
      <line x1="420" y1="120" x2="420" y2="210" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
      <text x="420" y="235" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fbbf24" font-size="10">Gap grows</text>
      <text x="420" y="246" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fbbf24" font-size="10">exponentially</text>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
    <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border-subtle rounded-xl p-5">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary mb-2">💊 Drug Discovery</h3>
      <p class="text-text-muted text-xs">Simulating molecular interactions to find new drugs. A caffeine molecule needs ~10⁴⁸ classical states to fully simulate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border-subtle rounded-xl p-5">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary mb-2">🔐 Cryptography</h3>
      <p class="text-text-muted text-xs">Factoring a 2048-bit RSA key would take classical computers billions of years. Shor's algorithm could do it in hours.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border-subtle rounded-xl p-5">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary mb-2">📦 Optimization</h3>
      <p class="text-text-muted text-xs">Finding the best route among millions of possibilities. Logistics, finance, scheduling — combinatorial explosion.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Brief History Timeline -->
<section class="mb-12 md:mb-20 fade-in">
  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-6 text-white">A Brief History</h2>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 160" class="w-full h-auto min-w-[480px]">
      <!-- Timeline line -->
      <line x1="30" y1="80" x2="570" y2="80" stroke="#262626" stroke-width="2"/>

      <!-- 1981 Feynman -->
      <circle cx="60" cy="80" r="6" fill="#00FF88"/>
      <text x="60" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#00FF88" font-size="11" font-weight="600">1981</text>
      <text x="60" y="110" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A3A3A3" font-size="9">Feynman</text>
      <text x="60" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="8">proposes QC</text>

      <!-- 1994 Shor -->
      <circle cx="170" cy="80" r="6" fill="#a78bfa"/>
      <text x="170" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#a78bfa" font-size="11" font-weight="600">1994</text>
      <text x="170" y="110" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A3A3A3" font-size="9">Shor's</text>
      <text x="170" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="8">Algorithm</text>

      <!-- 1996 Grover -->
      <circle cx="220" cy="80" r="6" fill="#60a5fa"/>
      <text x="220" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#60a5fa" font-size="11" font-weight="600">1996</text>
      <text x="220" y="110" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A3A3A3" font-size="9">Grover's</text>
      <text x="220" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="8">Search</text>

      <!-- 2001 First factoring -->
