<!-- Section 16: Deutsch-Jozsa Algorithm -->
<section class="mb-12 md:mb-20 fade-in">
  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2 text-white">Deutsch-Jozsa Algorithm</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted text-sm mb-6">Part 4: Quantum Algorithms</p>

  <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border-subtle rounded-xl p-6 md:p-8">
    <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm mb-4">
      <strong class="text-white">The problem:</strong> You have a black-box function f(x) that takes n-bit
      inputs and outputs 0 or 1. You're promised it's either <em>constant</em> (same output for all inputs)
      or <em>balanced</em> (outputs 0 for exactly half, 1 for the other half). Which is it?
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
      <div class="bg-bg-code rounded-lg p-4">
        <p class="text-red-400 font-semibold text-sm mb-2">Classical</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted text-xs">Need 2ⁿ⁻¹ + 1 queries in worst case. For n=100, that's ~10³⁰ evaluations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-bg-code rounded-lg p-4">
        <p class="text-primary font-semibold text-sm mb-2">Quantum</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted text-xs">Needs exactly <strong class="text-white">1 query</strong>. One. Regardless of n.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Circuit SVG -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 600 180" class="w-full h-auto min-w-[480px]">
        <!-- Input qubits -->
        <text x="30" y="55" fill="#00FF88" font-size="12">|0⟩ⁿ</text>
        <line x1="65" y1="50" x2="530" y2="50" stroke="#404040" stroke-width="1.5"/>

        <text x="30" y="125" fill="#00FF88" font-size="12">|1⟩</text>
        <line x1="65" y1="120" x2="530" y2="120" stroke="#404040" stroke-width="1.5"/>

        <!-- H gates -->
        <rect x="100" y="30" width="45" height="40" rx="4" fill="#1a1a1a" stroke="#a78bfa" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="122" y="55" text-anchor="middle" fill="#a78bfa" font-size="14" font-weight="600">H⊗ⁿ</text>

        <rect x="100" y="100" width="45" height="40" rx="4" fill="#1a1a1a" stroke="#a78bfa" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="122" y="125" text-anchor="middle" fill="#a78bfa" font-size="14" font-weight="600">H</text>

        <!-- Oracle -->
        <rect x="210" y="25" width="80" height="120" rx="6" fill="#1a1a1a" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="250" y="75" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fbbf24" font-size="14" font-weight="600">Uf</text>
        <text x="250" y="92" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="9">Oracle</text>

        <!-- H gates again -->
        <rect x="360" y="30" width="45" height="40" rx="4" fill="#1a1a1a" stroke="#a78bfa" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="382" y="55" text-anchor="middle" fill="#a78bfa" font-size="14" font-weight="600">H⊗ⁿ</text>

        <!-- Measurement -->
        <rect x="460" y="30" width="40" height="40" rx="4" fill="#1a1a1a" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <path d="M 470 60 Q 480 42 490 60" fill="none" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="480" y1="50" x2="485" y2="40" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="1.5"/>

        <!-- Labels -->
        <text x="300" y="170" text-anchor="middle" fill="#525252" font-size="10">All zeros → constant | Any non-zero → balanced</text>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm mt-4">
      The algorithm works by creating superposition over all inputs, querying the oracle once,
      then using interference to reveal whether the function is constant or balanced. The answer
      appears deterministically — no probabilistic repetition needed.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: Grover's Search -->
<section class="mb-12 md:mb-20 fade-in">
  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-6 text-white">Grover's Search Algorithm</h2>

  <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border-subtle rounded-xl p-6 md:p-8">
    <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm mb-4">
      <strong class="text-white">The problem:</strong> Search an unsorted database of N items for a specific one.
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
      <div class="bg-bg-code rounded-lg p-4">
        <p class="text-red-400 font-semibold text-sm mb-2">Classical: O(N)</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted text-xs">Check items one by one. On average N/2 checks.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-bg-code rounded-lg p-4">
        <p class="text-primary font-semibold text-sm mb-2">Quantum: O(√N)</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted text-xs">~√N iterations. For 1 million items: 1000 vs 500,000 checks.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white mb-4">Amplitude Amplification</h3>
    <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm mb-6">
      Grover's algorithm works by repeatedly applying two operations: (1) mark the correct answer
      by flipping its amplitude sign, (2) amplify marked amplitudes using "inversion about the mean."
      After ~√N iterations, the correct answer's amplitude is close to 1.
    </p>

    <!-- Amplitude amplification SVG -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
